{"ts": 1789770914.8003168, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "This is the key diagnostic, and it changes my read considerably.\n\nFeatures 13 and 39 individually scored 0.441 and 0.372. Combined without 18 they give 0.573, and adding 32 gives 0.689. If 13 and 39 were pure noise, combining two independent noise features should give \u2248 (0.441+0.372)/\u221a2\u00b7(something) \u2014 but critically, **combining uncorrelated features each with modest true signal is exactly how you get \u221an improvement**. 0.573 \u2248 0.813/\u221a2 \u00d7 1.0 is consistent with near-zero correlation between them.\n\nSo the honest interpretation is ambiguous between two stories:\n- **13 and 39 carry weak real signal** (their combination compounding as expected), or\n- **they're noise** and I selected them precisely because they topped a 40-feature scan\n\nThe scan is the problem. With 40 features \u00d7 2 signs, the max of ~80 noise draws at SE\u22480.22 has expected value around 0.22\u00d72.5 \u2248 0.55. Features 13 (0.441) and 39 (0.372) sit *comfortably inside* that null distribution. Feature 18 at 0.870 sits about 4 SE out \u2014 that one is real. The others are not distinguishable from selection artifacts.\n\nLet me verify 18's robustness rather than search for more lift."}
