{"ts": 1789770931.082568, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "This confirms the mechanism precisely. Pairing 18 with features that scored \u22480 individually (4, 28, 33, 30) gives \u22480.60 \u2248 0.870/\u221a2. That is the exact dilution signature of adding independent noise \u2014 the signal gets halved in weight while variance only drops by \u221a2.\n\nSo when features 13/39/32 paired with 18 gave 0.92 instead of 0.60, that *wasn't* dilution \u2014 those features contributed something. The gain over 0.60 is real information. But here's the catch: I found them by scanning 40 features and taking the top of the distribution, so their contribution is inflated by selection. The truth is somewhere between \"noise\" and \"as good as it looks in-sample.\"\n\nMy best in-sample spec is `[18, 13, 39]` at 0.966, still under the 1.028 bar. I could keep searching sign flips and combinations to cross it \u2014 but that would only mean I'd found a more overfit specification. Crossing a search-adjusted bar by searching harder is self-defeating: the bar exists to price in exactly that behavior.\n\nI'll submit the triple, since the diversification benefit is structurally real, but with a forecast that heavily discounts the selection effect.\n\n**My reasoning for the prediction:**\n- Feature 18 is genuinely predictive: ~4 SE from zero, survives sign reversal symmetrically, and its standalone 0.87 is not attainable by chance in an 80-draw scan.\n- Features 13 and 39 are *selected maxima* of a 40-feature scan. Their in-sample 0.44/0.37 lie within the null's expected maximum (~0.55), so I shrink their true contribution close to zero.\n- Expected OOS: feature 18 delivers ~0.75\u20130.85 alone after mild shrinkage. Equal-weighting it with two mostly-noise features costs dilution (toward ~0.60) but recovers some via any genuine 13/39 signal. Net center of mass \u2248 **0.70**.\n- Uncertainty is wide: sampling SE alone is ~0.22, plus regime/structural risk and the unresolved question of how much of 13/39 is real. I'll use **sd 0.30**."}
