{"ts": 1789770990.7026508, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted:** features [18, 13, 39], all long. In-sample Sharpe 0.966. Predicted OOS: **0.70 \u00b1 0.30**.\n\n### What the data showed\n\nScanning all 40 features individually, one result stood apart: **feature 18 at 0.870**. Everything else fell within \u00b10.44 \u2014 a band fully consistent with noise, since at n=5000 the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is \u2248 \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.22.\n\nThe decisive experiment was pairing 18 with features that had scored \u22480 on their own (4, 28, 33, 30). All four landed at \u22480.60 \u2248 0.870/\u221a2 \u2014 the textbook dilution signature of averaging a real signal with independent noise. This gave me a calibrated null: **any pair with 18 that scores ~0.60 added nothing.**\n\nAgainst that yardstick, pairs with 13, 39, and 32 scoring ~0.88\u20130.92 *did* contribute genuine independent information. Supporting this, 13 and 39 combined without 18 gave 0.573 \u2014 the \u221a2 compounding you'd expect from two uncorrelated signals, not from noise.\n\n### Why I stopped short of the bar\n\nMy best spec sits at 0.966 against a search-adjusted bar of **1.028**. I could almost certainly cross it by grinding through more sign flips and triples \u2014 but that would be self-defeating. The bar is explicitly search-adjusted, so clearing it *by searching harder* produces a more overfit specification, not a better one. The honest move was to stop once I understood the structure.\n\n### How I set the forecast\n\nI deliberately predicted **well below** my 0.966 in-sample number:\n\n- Feature 18 is real \u2014 ~4 SE from zero, beyond what a 40-feature \u00d7 2-sign scan (~80 draws, expected max \u2248 0.55) can manufacture. It should survive at ~0.75\u20130.85.\n- Features 13 and 39 are **selected maxima**. Their in-sample scores of 0.44 and 0.37 fall *inside* the null's expected maximum, so I shrank their true contribution close to zero regardless of the encouraging pair evidence.\n- Equal-weighting forces dilution of my one reliable signal, pulling toward ~0.60, with partial recovery if 13/39 are genuine.\n\nThe sd of 0.30 reflects that sampling error (~0.22) is only part of the risk \u2014 the unresolved question of how much of 13/39 is real is an irreducible uncertainty I can't settle from in-sample data alone.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: equal-weighting is a real constraint here. Feature 18 alone (0.870) may well beat this triple out-of-sample, since the combination spends two-thirds of its weight on features I can't confirm. If concentration were acceptable, the single-feature spec would be the more defensible bet."}
